WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.223
The hardest part of building an Office alternative is making documents look the same as they do in Microsoft Office.

2
00:00:06.343 --> 00:00:08.371
Reading the file is only the beginning.

3
00:00:08.491 --> 00:00:13.241
Fonts, line breaks, chart labels, and page boundaries must agree.

4
00:00:13.361 --> 00:00:17.803
Here is how we find those differences and fix them, one document at a time.

5
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:22.864
We build a broad collection of documents, presentations, and spreadsheets.

6
00:00:22.984 --> 00:00:31.688
It needs ordinary layouts and awkward edge cases: floating pictures, tables, chart themes, empty paragraphs, and printing settings.

7
00:00:31.808 --> 00:00:36.855
A simple document may look right while a more complicated one exposes a rule we have missed.

8
00:00:37.235 --> 00:00:43.321
We let Word, PowerPoint, and Excel render those files, then print or export them as PDFs.

9
00:00:43.441 --> 00:00:47.050
Those Microsoft PDFs are our source of truth for appearance.

10
00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:52.896
We keep the reference files and their rendering settings, so repeated comparisons have a stable target.

11
00:00:53.016 --> 00:00:57.384
Starling then renders exactly the same input files into its own PDFs.

12
00:00:57.764 --> 00:01:00.191
The repository automates both sides.

13
00:01:00.311 --> 00:01:06.940
PowerPoint exports through AppleScript, Word uses its print dialog, and Excel has a Windows export script.

14
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.232
Starling exports through command-line options for each app.

15
00:01:10.352 --> 00:01:15.156
These examples use the saved Microsoft references and actual Starling outputs.

16
00:01:15.276 --> 00:01:20.391
Now we can compare complete pages without manually switching between two applications.

17
00:01:20.771 --> 00:01:22.685
This is the comparison command.

18
00:01:22.805 --> 00:01:28.340
It rasterizes both PDFs at the same resolution, then compares corresponding pixels.

19
00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:33.437
In this run, a pixel turns red when any color channel differs by more than thirty-two.

20
00:01:33.557 --> 00:01:38.954
The output contains the Microsoft page, the Starling page, and a red difference mask.

21
00:01:39.074 --> 00:01:42.884
Fonts and rasterization can also contribute to the red pixels.

22
00:01:43.264 --> 00:01:45.396
Here is the earlier chart export.

23
00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:49.022
Compare the colors, title, labels, and legend.

24
00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:52.939
The red mask marks thirteen point zero five percent of the page.

25
00:01:53.059 --> 00:01:56.922
It tells us where the images disagree; it does not tell us why.

26
00:01:57.042 --> 00:02:01.488
To find the cause, we inspect the document structure and the code that draws it.

27
00:02:01.868 --> 00:02:05.665
The chart has its own theme, separate from the presentation theme.

28
00:02:05.785 --> 00:02:08.068
Reading that override fixes the colors.

29
00:02:08.188 --> 00:02:13.084
Other changes restore title formatting, labels from cells, and legend layout.

30
00:02:13.204 --> 00:02:15.977
These are separate, reviewable code changes.

31
00:02:16.097 --> 00:02:21.254
We fix the general rendering rules, so other files using those rules can benefit too.

32
00:02:21.634 --> 00:02:25.195
Export the file again and run exactly the same comparison.

33
00:02:25.315 --> 00:02:30.667
The current chart is much closer, and the differing pixels drop to two point zero eight percent.

34
00:02:30.787 --> 00:02:32.067
Some red remains.

35
00:02:32.187 --> 00:02:37.120
That is the next investigation, rather than a reason to declare perfect compatibility.

36
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:41.046
These outputs span several improvements, not just one patch.

37
00:02:41.426 --> 00:02:44.116
A smaller difference can affect a whole document.

38
00:02:44.236 --> 00:02:47.481
On page four, Starling starts the text too low.

39
00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:53.256
The cause is on the preceding page: blank lines containing spaces and tabs are measured too tall.

40
00:02:53.376 --> 00:02:56.838
Word sizes those lines using the smaller paragraph mark.

41
00:02:56.958 --> 00:03:00.079
The extra height accumulates and shifts later content.

42
00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:05.193
After correcting that rule, the heading and table return to their expected positions.

43
00:03:05.313 --> 00:03:09.256
Here are the actual before and after difference masks for the same region.

44
00:03:09.376 --> 00:03:15.375
Across page four, differing pixels fall from seven point five zero to three point eight one percent.

45
00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:18.129
The extra fifth page also disappears.

46
00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:21.113
Remaining text differences still need inspection.

47
00:03:21.493 --> 00:03:23.567
Excel gives us another example.

48
00:03:23.687 --> 00:03:27.554
The reference prints category names and percentages outside the pie.

49
00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:31.330
The earlier Starling export puts values inside the slices.

50
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.497
Reading the label content, placement, and formatting improves this.

51
00:03:35.617 --> 00:03:40.840
Printing changes also matter: the earlier output is missing a page present in the reference.

52
00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.816
Here is the current page and its difference mask.

53
00:03:43.936 --> 00:03:48.426
Page one improves from sixteen point five three to three point four two percent.

54
00:03:48.546 --> 00:03:51.312
Some label text and percentages still differ.

55
00:03:51.432 --> 00:03:54.660
The mask helps us choose the next case to investigate.

56
00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:01.070
A rendering comparison measures appearance; formulas, editing, and saving back need their own checks.

57
00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.399
Then we rerun the wider collection.

58
00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:09.100
A proposed print-area rule once improved one spreadsheet but added eleven unwanted pages to another.

59
00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.658
That change was reverted.

60
00:04:10.778 --> 00:04:16.061
The collection keeps us honest: a local improvement is not enough if it breaks other documents.

61
00:04:16.181 --> 00:04:19.068
Every mismatch gives us evidence to refine the rule.

62
00:04:19.448 --> 00:04:28.386
That is the daily loop: document, Microsoft reference, Starling export, diff, investigation, fix, and another comparison.

63
00:04:28.506 --> 00:04:36.819
The history shows printing fixes, chart formatting, floating pictures, blank-line heights, and chart labels progressing across the week.

64
00:04:36.939 --> 00:04:42.061
We keep repeating the process, including the small differences that are easy to overlook.

65
00:04:42.441 --> 00:04:46.292
Our goal is simple: your document should look the way its author intended.

66
00:04:46.412 --> 00:04:47.804
There is still work ahead.

67
00:04:47.924 --> 00:04:54.178
A small file you can share, together with its expected Microsoft output, can become the next test.

68
00:04:54.298 --> 00:04:58.754
That is how we improve compatibility, one reproducible difference at a time.
